A reviewer claims this correctness issue:
  title: NaN propagated into raw SQL string
  lines: 3-4 (new file)
  rationale: Number(id) returns NaN when id is undefined, a non-numeric string (e.g. "abc"), or any other non-coercible value. On line 4, NaN is concatenated into the SQL string, producing "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = NaN", which is invalid SQL. The database will throw an error rather than returning an empty result or a well-typed error, so the function produces a broken outcome for this entire class of inputs.
Number(null), Number(''), Number(false), and Number([]) all return 0. byId(null) therefore executes "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 0", which either returns an unintended row (if one exists) or an empty result, with no indication that the caller passed a meaningless id. This is a silent wrong-result bug rather than an error.

The ONLY context you may use is this hunk:
File: lookup.ts
Hunk: @@ -1,1 +1,4 @@
Lines show NEW-file line numbers; + added, - removed, space unchanged.
   1   class Lookup {
   2 +   byId(id) {
   3 +     const safeId = Number(id);
   4 +     return this.db.raw("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = " + safeId);
   5 +   }

Is the finding real and supported by THIS hunk? Try to refute it before agreeing. Report your outcome (upheld/refuted), your confidence, and your reasoning.
